{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01F32tRC8hta14HBevX8pENo","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /tmp/claude-eval-4pn8gE/home/cwd/SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md has been updated. All occurrences were successfully replaced. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"540eadae-cfac-44bd-ab67-b1e1e5565f35","uuid":"d9ba070d-441a-4332-982b-a265cbcc02dd","timestamp":"2026-11-10T08:16:11.206Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/tmp/claude-eval-4pn8gE/home/cwd/SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md","oldString":"(#d2-processing-model)","newString":"(#d2-processing-model-proposed)","originalFile":"# Source of Truth — Itinerary PDF Export by Email

**Version** 0.1 · **Status** Draft, awaiting confirmation of `(proposed)` items · **Updated** 2026-11-10

Related: [ARCHITECTURE_ROADMAP.md](ARCHITECTURE_ROADMAP.md) (schema, API, phases) · [TODO_WORKFLOW.md](TODO_WORKFLOW.md) (tasks) · [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md) (rules + sync protocol)

If anything in another doc or in code conflicts with this file, this file wins.
Anything marked `(proposed)` is a choice made without user input and is not final until confirmed.

## 1. Scope

A signed-in user, viewing a trip they own, presses one button. The server renders that trip's itinerary as a PDF and emails it, as an attachment, to the user's own account email address.

### Non-goals (v1)

| Non-goal | Why |
|---|---|
| Sending to any address other than the user's account email | "Email it to themselves." An arbitrary recipient turns the feature into a spam relay. |
| Storing generated PDFs | No blob storage, no retention policy to own. The email is the copy. |
| Direct PDF download in the browser | Not requested. Cheap to add later: the renderer is a pure function ([D4](#d4-pdf-rendering)). |
| Sharing with other travellers, scheduled sends, custom templates/branding | Not requested. |
| Bounce / delivery tracking after the provider accepts the message | Needs provider webhooks. See [Q5](#5-open-questions). |
| Background job queue / worker | See [D2](#d2-processing-model). |

## 2. Domain model

| Entity | What it is | Owned by this feature? |
|---|---|---|
| `User` | Account with one email address and a verified-at timestamp. | No — owned by auth ([Q1](#5-open-questions)). The feature needs only `id`, `email`, `email_verified_at`. |
| `Trip` | A titled trip with a date range, owned by exactly one user. | No — app foundation. A minimal version is specified because nothing exists yet. |
| `ItineraryItem` | One entry on one day of a trip: title, optional time, location, notes, order within the day. | No — app foundation, minimal version specified. |
| `ItineraryExport` | One request to render and email one trip to its owner. Records recipient snapshot, status, and failure reason. Never holds the PDF. | **Yes.** |

Dates and times on itinerary items are **trip-local wall-clock values** (a `date` and an optional `time`), with no timezone conversion anywhere. The PDF prints them exactly as stored.

### Export lifecycle

```
            insert
              │
              ▼
         processing ──────────► sent      (provider accepted the message)
              │
              └───────────────► failed    (error_code says why)
```

`sent` and `failed` are terminal. A failed export is never retried in place; the user presses the button again, which creates a new export.

## 3. Invariants

Each invariant is enforced in the service layer **and** in Postgres where Postgres can express it. Where only one boundary is possible, that is stated rather than papered over. Column and constraint details live in [roadmap §2](ARCHITECTURE_ROADMAP.md#2-data-schema).

| # | Invariant | App boundary | DB boundary |
|---|---|---|---|
| I1 | Only a trip's owner can export it. | Service loads the trip by `(id, owner_id = session user)`; no match → 404. | Composite FK `itinerary_exports(trip_id, user_id) → trips(id, owner_id)`. |
| I2 | The recipient is the user's account email, read server-side at request time. It never comes from the client. | Request schema is strict and has no recipient field; service copies `users.email`. | `recipient_email NOT NULL` snapshot column. Only the service writes it. |
| I3 | Email is sent only to a verified address. | Service rejects when `email_verified_at IS NULL`. | None — app only. Depends on auth ([Q1](#5-open-questions)). |
| I4 | At most one in-flight export per trip. | Service maps the unique violation to `409 export_in_progress`. | Partial unique index on `(trip_id) WHERE status = 'processing'`. |
| I5 | Repeating a request with the same idempotency key never creates a second export or a second email. | Service looks up the key first and returns the existing export. | `UNIQUE (user_id, idempotency_key)`. |
| I6 | Status moves only `processing → sent` or `processing → failed`. | Every status write is a conditional `UPDATE … WHERE status = 'processing'`; zero rows updated means someone else finished it, and the writer stops. | `CHECK` on allowed values; `CHECK`s tying `finished_at` and `error_code` to status. |
| I7 | An empty itinerary is never exported. | Service counts items; zero → 422. | `item_count INT NOT NULL CHECK (item_count > 0)` on the export row. |
| I8 | A user cannot create more than the rate limit of exports per window. | Service counts the user's recent exports before insert. | None — app only. A concurrent burst across *different* trips can overshoot by a few; I4 bounds it per trip. Accepted. |
| I9 | User-supplied text (titles, notes, locations) is rendered as plain text, never interpreted as markup, and never reaches an email header unsanitised. | Renderer takes text nodes only ([D4](#d4-pdf-rendering)); subject and filename are built from a sanitised title. | n/a |

## 4. Decisions

### D1. Recipient is fixed to the account email
- **Decision:** No recipient input anywhere in the API or UI.
- **Why:** Matches the ask; removes the abuse surface and the need for recipient validation.
- **Rejected:** Free-form "send to" field.

> Do not add a recipient field without an explicit decision. It changes the threat model (I2).

### D2. Processing model `(proposed)`
- **Decision:** Render and send **inside the POST request**. The export row is written first (`processing`), then updated to `sent` or `failed` before the response returns.
- **Why:** One trip's PDF and one provider call should take seconds. No queue, worker, or cron to deploy and monitor. The row already gives idempotency, rate limiting, and an audit trail.
- **Rejected:** Postgres-backed job queue with a worker. More moving parts, and it needs a hosting answer ([Q2](#5-open-questions)) that isn't known.
- **Revisit when:** measured request time approaches the host's request timeout, or automatic retries across minutes are wanted. The state machine extends with a `pending` status; the API contract does not change.

### D3. PDFs are not stored
