## Findings

| # | Severity | Line | Issue |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Critical on `jsonwebtoken` ≤ 8.5.1, otherwise low | 6 | Unset or empty `JWT_SECRET` is never checked |
| 2 | High if the secret is shared, otherwise medium | 6 | No issuer, audience or token-type check |
| 3 | Medium–high | 6 | Expiry is not required, and there is no revocation |
| 4 | Medium | 6 | Auth failures throw, giving a 500 and a possible stack trace |
| 5 | Medium if the secret is weak | 6 | No minimum secret strength |
| 6 | Low today | 6 | Algorithms are not pinned |
| 7 | Low | 7 | Raw payload becomes `req.user` |
| 8 | Low, not exploitable | 5 | Loose `Bearer` parsing |

1. **Missing secret.** On `jsonwebtoken` ≤ 8.5.1, a falsy secret with no `algorithms` option makes `verify` accept an unsigned `alg: none` token (CVE-2022-23540). Anyone can then be any user. On v9+ it fails closed and every request is rejected. No version is pinned, so which case you are in is undetermined.

2. **No `iss`/`aud`/type check.** Any token signed with the same secret is accepted as an access token: staging tokens, another service's tokens, refresh or password-reset JWTs. All three reviewers flagged this; it is the most realistic risk if the secret is reused anywhere.

3. **Expiry optional.** `verify` only checks `exp` if the claim is present, so a token minted without it is valid forever. A stolen token replays until you rotate the secret.

4. **Uncaught throw.** A missing, malformed or expired token makes `verify` throw. Express catches it, so it fails closed, but the client gets a 500 rather than a 401. Unless `NODE_ENV=production`, the response body includes the stack trace, with file paths and the exact failure reason.

5. **Weak secret.** The library enforces no minimum HMAC key length. Anyone holding one valid token can brute-force a short secret offline and then forge arbitrary claims.

6. **Algorithms not pinned.** With a string HMAC secret this only means HS256, HS384 and HS512 are all accepted. It becomes serious only if the env var ever holds a PEM public key on an old library version.

7. **Unvalidated `req.user`.** The payload can be a string for a non-JSON token, and every claim flows straight to handlers. Roles in the token are also a snapshot from mint time, so a demoted user keeps old privileges until expiry.

8. **Scheme parsing.** `replace('Bearer ', '')` is unanchored and accepts a bare token with no scheme. Every accepted path still needs a validly signed token, so there is no bypass.

One thing outside the file: `requireAuth` is not mounted in `src/app.js` yet. Any route registered before it will be silently unprotected, and mounted globally it will reject CORS preflights.

## Suggested fix

This closes 1–8 except revocation, which needs a denylist or per-user token version if logout must take effect. The issuer and audience values are placeholders, and your token issuer must set matching `iss`, `aud`, `exp` and `sub` claims.

```js
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken'); // pin ^9 in package.json

const SECRET = process.env.JWT_SECRET;
if (!SECRET || Buffer.byteLength(SECRET) < 32) {
  throw new Error('JWT_SECRET must be set and at least 32 bytes');
}

function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const m = /^Bearer +(\S+)$/i.exec(req.headers.authorization || '');
  try {
    if (!m) throw new Error('no bearer token');
    const p = jwt.verify(m[1], SECRET, {
      algorithms: ['HS256'],
      issuer: 'your-issuer',
      audience: 'your-api',
      maxAge: '15m',
    });
    if (typeof p !== 'object' || typeof p.sub !== 'string' || !p.exp) {
      throw new Error('bad claims');
    }
    req.user = { id: p.sub, role: p.role };
    next();
  } catch {
    res.set('WWW-Authenticate', 'Bearer').status(401).json({ error: 'unauthorized' });
  }
}

module.exports = { requireAuth };
```

